# Grade 11 (part b): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'11-3-2': dict(
  saint="Leopold Mandić: a tiny Capuchin who heard confessions for thirty years in a Padua cell.",
  tryit=["Walk through the rite of Reconciliation.", "Mark what the penitent does and what the priest does.", "Prepare an examination of conscience."],
  saintStory="Bogdan Mandić was born in 1866 in Herceg Novi, in present-day Montenegro. He was a tiny man, barely four feet five inches, with a speech impediment and weak health. He joined the Capuchins, taking the name Leopold, and was ordained in 1890. He dreamed of going to the East to work for the reunion of Orthodox and Catholics, but his superiors judged his health unfit, and he spent his life in Italy.\nHe was assigned to Padua, where he spent most of his priestly life in a small cell by the church, hearing confessions, for ten to fifteen hours a day. People of all kinds came, including priests and academics. He was gentle and patient, and many left in tears. When asked why he was so merciful, he said, “I give the people all the forgiveness that Jesus gave. If the Lord reproaches me for being too generous, I will say, ‘It was you who gave me such a bad example.’”\nHe died in 1942, and was canonized in 1983.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board, “Why say it out loud?” Give students sixty seconds to jot down the reasons they can think of for and against confessing to another person. Collect a few from each side.",
    teach=["Read John 20:19-23. Say: “On Easter night Jesus appeared to the apostles and said, ‘Peace be with you… Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven them; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained.’ This is the institution of the sacrament of Reconciliation (also called Penance or Confession). Notice that he gave the power to men, not just directly to God. The priest acts in persona Christi. Why a priest? Because sin is also against the Church; because we need to say it aloud; because we need to hear the words of absolution.”", "Say: “The acts of the penitent are contrition, confession and satisfaction. Contrition: sorrow of the soul and detestation of sin with the resolution not to sin again. Perfect contrition, from love of God, forgives venial sins immediately and mortal sins with the intention to confess. Imperfect contrition (attrition), from fear or the ugliness of sin, is enough for the sacrament. Confession: all mortal sins (in kind and number) must be confessed. Venial sins are good to confess. Satisfaction: the penance given by the priest to repair the harm and heal. The priest’s act is absolution: ‘God, the Father of mercies, through the death and resurrection of his Son has reconciled the world to himself and sent the Holy Spirit among us for the forgiveness of sins; through the ministry of the Church may God give you pardon and peace, and I absolve you from your sins in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’”", "Say: “Rite. Greeting; Sign of the Cross; reading of the Word (optional); confession of sins; the priest gives counsel and a penance; act of contrition; absolution; thanksgiving; dismissal. Face-to-face or behind a screen. Seal of confession: absolute; the priest can never reveal what he hears, even under threat of death. Effects: reconciliation with God and the Church, peace of conscience, spiritual consolation, forgiveness of eternal punishment for mortal sins, and a remission of temporal punishment. The Church requires confessing grave sins at least once a year, and before Communion if in mortal sin. Frequent confession is encouraged. Three forms of rite: individual, communal with individual confession, and general absolution (only in grave necessity). A good examination of conscience uses the Ten Commandments or the Beatitudes.”"],
    ask=["What are the acts of the penitent? (Contrition, confession, satisfaction.)", "What does the priest do? (Gives counsel and penance, absolves in the person of Christ.)", "What is the seal of confession? (The priest may never reveal what he hears.)"],
    practice=["Make a flowchart of the rite with who speaks at each step.", "Write an examination of conscience for your age based on the Ten Commandments (7-10 questions).", "Memorize the words of the Act of Contrition.", "Plan when you will go to Confession next."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition aloud together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Schedule a Confession this month.”")),

'11-3-3': dict(
  saint="John of God: a bookseller who turned a rented house into a hospital for the poor.",
  tryit=["Describe the Anointing in your own words.", "Pair each sign with an effect.", "Write a plan to visit someone who is ill."],
  saintStory="João Cidade was born in 1495 in Portugal, and as a young man was a soldier and shepherd. Around the age of forty, in Granada, Spain, he heard a sermon by John of Avila, and was so moved by his own sins that he cried out and beat himself publicly. People thought he was mad, and he was committed to an asylum, where he was treated harshly. The cruel treatment of patients stirred in him a determination to help the sick. John of Avila visited him and told him to take action.\nHe was released and opened a bookshop. In 1539 he rented a house in Granada and filled it with poor, sick people, begging for food and medicine for them. He did the work with his own hands. When the Royal Hospital caught fire in 1549, he is said to have carried patients out through the flames.\nHe died in 1550 on his knees, at fifty-five. The followers who gathered around him became the Brothers Hospitallers of St. John of God. He is the patron of hospitals, nurses and the sick.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud James 5:14-15 and then sit in silence for a few seconds. Say, “This is one of the shortest instructions for a sacrament in the New Testament. Notice who is called, what is done, and what is promised.”",
    teach=["Read James 5:14-15. Say: “‘Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the Church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord; and the prayer of faith will save the sick man, and the Lord will raise him up; and if he has committed sins, he will be forgiven.’ The sacrament of the Anointing of the Sick has its roots in Jesus’ healing ministry (Mark 6:13) and the apostles’ practice. The Council of Trent taught that this is a sacrament instituted by Christ. For centuries it was called ‘Extreme Unction’ and was given only to the dying; Vatican II restored the fuller understanding as for the seriously ill and elderly.”", "Say: “Who may receive it: any baptized Catholic who has reached the age of reason and is in danger from illness or old age. This includes before serious surgery, and those weakened by old age. It may be repeated if the illness worsens or recurs. The minister is a priest or bishop. The rite: laying on of hands in silence, then anointing the forehead and hands with the oil of the sick (blessed by the bishop at the Chrism Mass) with the words, ‘Through this holy anointing may the Lord in his love and mercy help you with the grace of the Holy Spirit. May the Lord who frees you from sin save you and raise you up.’ If possible, it is celebrated within Mass, with the community. For one near death, it is combined with Penance and Viaticum.”", "Say: “Effects: a gift of the Holy Spirit, strengthening, peace and courage against the difficulties of illness; union with the Passion of Christ for the good of oneself and the Church; forgiveness of sins if the sick person was unable to confess; restoration of health, if it is conducive to salvation; preparation for the passage to eternal life. Sickness isn’t a punishment for sin (John 9:3); it’s an occasion for grace. The Church’s care for the sick continues Jesus’ ministry: hospitals began as Christian foundations. Visiting the sick is a work of mercy. The sacrament is often feared as ‘last rites’; it’s better to call the priest early. We should also support the caregivers. Viaticum, Communion for the dying, is ‘food for the journey.’”"],
    ask=["Who may receive the Anointing? (Baptized Catholics seriously ill or weakened by age.)", "What does the sacrament do? (Strengthens, forgives sins, unites with Christ’s suffering, may heal.)", "What is Viaticum? (Communion for the dying.)"],
    practice=["Match each sign (oil, laying on of hands, prayer of faith) with its effect and Bible text.", "Write a short explanation of the sacrament for a worried grandparent.", "Plan a visit, call or card to someone ill.", "Write a prayer for the sick."],
    close="Pray for the sick of your parish by name. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Visit or call someone who is ill.”")),

'11-4-1': dict(
  saint="Karl Leisner: a seminarian ordained secretly in Dachau and dying of tuberculosis soon after.",
  tryit=["Describe the three degrees of Holy Orders.", "Mark what is unique to each.", "Write a prayer for vocations."],
  saintStory="Karl Leisner was born in 1915 in Germany. As a young man he was active in Catholic youth groups, which Hitler’s regime tried to eliminate. He kept a diary. In 1939 he was ordained a deacon, but was soon arrested, after overhearing that he had said something critical of an assassination attempt on Hitler. He was sent to Dachau concentration camp, where he contracted tuberculosis.\nBy 1944, he was dying. A French bishop, Gabriel Piguet, was also a prisoner at Dachau. Plans were made to smuggle in what was needed. On December 17, 1944, in the camp chapel, Bishop Piguet ordained Karl Leisner a priest, with a few trusted prisoners present. A fellow prisoner made the vestments secretly, and the ordination oil and the ring were smuggled in. A few days later, on December 26, Karl celebrated his first and only Mass.\nKarl was freed in 1945 when the camp was liberated, but died of tuberculosis in August. He was twenty-nine. He was beatified in 1996. He shows what the priesthood means: a call to offer Mass at any cost.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If the Church were short of priests, could we simply choose a faithful layman to celebrate Mass? What would be missing?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Holy Orders is ‘the sacrament through which the mission entrusted by Christ to his apostles continues to be exercised in the Church until the end of time.’ (CCC 1536) It has three degrees: episcopate, presbyterate, diaconate. The bishop is the fullness of the priesthood; he teaches, sanctifies and governs. Priests are co-workers of the bishop; they preach, celebrate the Eucharist and other sacraments, and lead parishes. Deacons are ordained ‘not unto priesthood but unto ministry’: they proclaim the Gospel, preach, assist at Mass, baptize, witness marriages, and serve in charity. Only bishops can ordain.”", "Say: “The rite. The essential rite is the laying on of hands by the bishop and the consecratory prayer asking God for the outpouring of the Holy Spirit. For a priest, the ordinand is presented, elected, examined, promises obedience; the Litany of the Saints is sung while he lies prostrate; the bishop lays on hands in silence; then the prayer of ordination. Explanatory rites: anointing of the hands with chrism, giving the paten and chalice, the vestments (stole and chasuble). Effects: a special configuration to Christ as Priest, Teacher and Shepherd, and a permanent character; one cannot be ‘un-ordained,’ though one may be dispensed from obligations.”", "Say: “Who may be ordained: baptized men called by God and the Church. The Church holds that she has no authority to ordain women (John Paul II, Ordinatio Sacerdotalis). In the Latin Church, priests are normally celibate; Eastern Catholic priests may be married. Candidates go through years of formation in a seminary: human, spiritual, intellectual and pastoral. A vocation is a call from God, confirmed by the Church. The priest acts in persona Christi capitis. Though the priest is an ordinary man with weaknesses, the sacraments are valid regardless. Pope Benedict XVI said that the priesthood is ‘the love of the heart of Jesus.’ We pray for vocations: ‘Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest.’ (Matthew 9:38) We also support our priests: with prayer, friendship, and respect.”"],
    ask=["What are the three degrees of Holy Orders? (Bishop, priest, deacon.)", "What is the essential rite? (Laying on of hands and the prayer of consecration by the bishop.)", "Why can’t ordination be undone? (It confers an indelible character.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Bishop, Priest, Deacon with columns for role, faculties, and Scripture roots.", "Write a letter of thanks to a priest or deacon, mentioning something specific.", "Compose a prayer for vocations.", "Pray it daily this week."],
    close="Pray: “Lord of the harvest, send laborers.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray daily for vocations.”")),

'11-4-2': dict(
  saint="Luigi and Maria Beltrame Quattrocchi: a married couple beatified together who opened their home in wartime.",
  tryit=["Outline the properties and ends of marriage.", "Check what makes a marriage valid.", "Draft a prayer for your future marriage."],
  saintStory="Luigi Beltrame Quattrocchi (1880–1951) was a lawyer, and Maria Corsini (1884–1965) was a teacher and writer, in Rome. They married in 1905 and had four children. One pregnancy was complicated; doctors advised that the baby could not be saved and Maria’s life was in danger. They trusted God, and their daughter Enrichetta was born healthy.\nThe couple lived their faith in daily life. They prayed the Rosary and went to Mass together, and their home was open to friends, relatives and the needy. Three of their children entered religious life: two became priests, and one a Trappist nun. During World War II, the family hid Jews and other refugees in their home. Maria also worked with the Red Cross, was involved in Catholic Action, and helped to found a school.\nThey were beatified together in 2001, the first married couple in history to be beatified as a couple. Pope John Paul II said that they lived “ordinary life in an extraordinary way.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand each pair a blank card. Ask them to write, in one line each, what they think the most important promise in a wedding is. Compare the cards around the room and look for patterns.",
    teach=["Read Genesis 2:24 and Matthew 19:4-6. Say: “‘Have you not read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female, and said, “For this reason a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one”? So they are no longer two but one. What therefore God has joined together, let not man put asunder.’ The Church teaches that marriage is a covenant by which a man and a woman establish between themselves a partnership of the whole of life, ordered by its nature to the good of the spouses and to the procreation and education of offspring. Between baptized persons, it has been raised by Christ to the dignity of a sacrament.”", "Say: “The essential properties: unity (one man and one woman) and indissolubility (permanent until death). The ends: the good of the spouses (unitive) and the procreation and education of children. The consent is what makes the marriage: ‘an act of the will by which a man and a woman mutually give and accept each other.’ (CCC 1625) It must be free, full, faithful, and fruitful. The spouses themselves are the ministers of the sacrament in the Latin Church; the priest or deacon is the official witness. A Catholic marriage should be in the Church, with the proper canonical form. The rite: the entrance, liturgy of the Word, the questions about freedom, fidelity and openness to children, the exchange of vows, the blessing and exchange of rings, the nuptial blessing, and usually a Mass.”", "Say: “The grace of the sacrament perfects the couple’s love, strengthens their indissoluble unity, and helps them to holiness. Paul calls marriage a great mystery that signifies Christ’s love for the Church (Ephesians 5:25-32). The couple become a sign of that love. Preparation: engaged couples undergo pastoral preparation, often a course of six months, including natural family planning, communication and finances. Impediments: age, impotence, a prior bond, disparity of cult (marrying an unbaptized person requires a dispensation), blood relationship, and others. The Church also recognizes marriages between non-Catholics and the Pauline privilege. Marriage is a vocation. Conditions for a good marriage: faith, communication, forgiveness, generosity, prayer. The family is the ‘domestic church.’”"],
    ask=["What are the properties of marriage? (Unity and indissolubility.)", "What makes a marriage? (The free consent of the spouses.)", "Who are the ministers in the Latin Church? (The spouses themselves.)"],
    practice=["Create a checklist for valid Catholic marriage: baptized or dispensation, free consent, proper form, no impediments, openness to children and permanence.", "Write the vows you would want to make in your own words.", "Write a prayer for your future spouse or vocation.", "Share in pairs."],
    close="Pray for married couples and engaged couples. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a married couple about their wedding vows.”")),

'11-4-3': dict(
  saint="Louis IX of France: a king, husband and father of eleven who also served lepers at his table.",
  tryit=["Describe the domestic church.", "List what makes a home a school of faith.", "Choose a family practice to start."],
  saintStory="Louis IX was born in 1214 and became king of France at twelve, with his mother Blanche of Castile as regent. She taught him that she would rather see him dead than commit a mortal sin. He married Margaret of Provence in 1234 and they had eleven children. He was devoted to his wife and children, and took care of their religious education.\nHe was a just king. He is said to have sat under an oak at Vincennes, hearing the petitions of any who came. He established a system of appeals, forbade trial by combat, and cared for the poor. Each day he heard Mass, prayed the Hours, and fed the poor at his table, sometimes serving lepers with his own hands. He built the Sainte-Chapelle in Paris to house the crown of thorns.\nHe went on two crusades, was captured on the first, and died of disease on the second, in Tunis in 1270. He is the patron of tertiaries and French kings. He showed how a lay person in public life can pursue holiness in marriage, family and office.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a house on the board and label the door ‘family.’ Say, “If this house were a church, what rooms would it need?” Take suggestions (a table, a place to pray, a place to forgive) and draw them in.",
    teach=["Say: “The Council called the family the ‘domestic church’ (LG 11). The Catechism: ‘The Christian home is the place where children receive the first proclamation of the faith.’ (CCC 1656) Family life is a school of virtue and faith. Parents are the first teachers of their children. They pass on faith by prayer together, by the sacraments, by example, by hospitality, by forgiving one another. Gaudium et Spes calls the family a ‘school of deeper humanity.’ John Paul II in Familiaris Consortio 17 spoke of the four tasks of the family: forming a community of persons, serving life, participating in society, and sharing in the Church’s life and mission.”", "Say: “Vocation: every person has a vocation; the word means ‘call.’ The first call is to holiness. The four states of life: marriage, ordained ministry, consecrated life (religious life, consecrated virgins, hermits), and the single life dedicated to God and others. Single people are not people without vocation. Some are single by circumstance; some by choice for service; some are ‘single for the Kingdom’ as lay missionaries. We discern vocation through prayer, the sacraments, honesty about gifts, advice from wise people, and the desires of the heart purified by God. God calls at times in surprising ways. Decisions about vocation should be made freely; no one should be pressured.”", "Say: “Trials. Families struggle with illness, financial stress, conflict, divorce, addiction and loss. The Church supports them. The sacraments strengthen. Forgiveness at home matters. Pope Francis in Amoris Laetitia (2016) says that love in the family is ‘patient, kind, not jealous,’ drawing on 1 Corinthians 13. Practices that help: a family meal with grace; Sunday Mass together; a family Rosary or night prayer; Advent wreath and Lenten traditions; blessing children; service together; talking about faith. Even if your family doesn’t practice, you can make your life a witness. You are also forming your future family now by the habits you build.”"],
    ask=["What is the domestic church? (The family as the first place of faith.)", "What are the four states of life? (Marriage, ordained ministry, consecrated life, dedicated single life.)", "What practices build a family’s faith? (Prayer, Sunday Mass, forgiveness, service, meals.)"],
    practice=["List five practices of a family that is a ‘domestic church.’", "Rate how many are in your home now, without judgment.", "Choose one small practice to begin this week.", "Write a prayer for your family."],
    close="Pray the Prayer for the Family or “Holy Family, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Start one family practice this week.”")),

'11-5-1': dict(
  saint="Jerzy Popiełuszko: a young Polish priest killed for preaching “overcome evil with good.”",
  tryit=["Explain why every person has dignity.", "Apply the Beatitudes to a modern setting.", "List what dignity rules out."],
  saintStory="Jerzy Popiełuszko was born in 1947 in a Polish farming village. He was a sickly boy, an altar server who walked miles to Mass. As a seminarian, he was drafted into the army, where he was harassed for his faith. He was ordained in 1972 and served in Warsaw.\nIn 1980, when the Solidarity movement was created, he celebrated Mass for striking steelworkers. During martial law in 1981, he began holding monthly “Masses for the Homeland” at St. Stanislaus Kostka Church in Warsaw, drawing tens of thousands. In his homilies, he preached the dignity of work, truth and freedom, and repeated Paul: “Overcome evil with good.” He always urged non-violence.\nThe communist security service harassed him, interrogated him, and tried to frame him. On October 19, 1984, three secret police officers kidnapped him and beat him to death. He was thirty-seven. A huge crowd of hundreds of thousands attended his funeral. He was beatified in 2010. His grave is one of Poland’s most visited.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board: “Why can’t I do whatever I want with another person?” Ask for the strongest reasons students can give, from any worldview. Then say, “The Church’s answer is a claim about what a human being is.”",
    teach=["Say: “The foundation of Catholic morality is human dignity. Genesis 1:27: ‘God created man in his own image.’ Each person is made in God’s image, has an intellect and free will, is capable of knowing and loving, and is called to communion with God. The Catechism: ‘The dignity of the human person is rooted in his creation in the image and likeness of God.’ (CCC 1700) It was elevated by the Incarnation: the Son took human nature. It is not earned by usefulness or taken away by weakness or sin. Human beings have rights because they have dignity, and duties because they are persons.”", "Say: “Our end is beatitude, happiness with God. The Beatitudes (Matthew 5:3-12) are the Church’s picture of the happy life. Each beatitude names a blessing and a promise. They are the heart of Jesus’ preaching and a portrait of his own life, and of the path of the saints. The Catechism says the Beatitudes ‘respond to the natural desire for happiness’ (CCC 1718) and ‘depict the countenance of Jesus Christ and portray his charity.’ They show the way: poverty of spirit, meekness, purity of heart, mercy, hunger for justice, peacemaking, perseverance under persecution. They invert the world’s ideas of success: they aren’t rules to follow to earn heaven, but a way of seeing and living under the Kingdom.”", "Say: “Implications. If every person has dignity, then life must be protected from conception to natural death; the poor and weak deserve special care; slavery, torture, human trafficking and racism are evils; so are using people as means. Dignity also means we have responsibilities: to seek the truth, to act freely and responsibly, to love. Popiełuszko lived this: he defended workers’ dignity by non-violent means, and paid with his life. The secret of the Beatitudes: Jesus lives them first. He was poor in spirit, meek, merciful, peacemaker, persecuted. When we live them, we are living in Christ. Pope Francis wrote, ‘The Beatitudes are like a Christian’s identity card.’ (Gaudete et Exsultate 63)”"],
    ask=["Where does human dignity come from? (Creation in God’s image, raised by the Incarnation.)", "What is our final end? (Beatitude, happiness with God.)", "What does Gaudete et Exsultate call the Beatitudes? (A Christian’s identity card.)"],
    practice=["Pick three Beatitudes and describe what each looks like at a school or workplace.", "List three things that violate human dignity, with one action to resist each.", "Write a one-paragraph answer to someone who says dignity depends on ability.", "Share one."],
    close="Pray the Beatitudes slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose a Beatitude to live this week.”")),

'11-5-2': dict(
  saint="Rupert Mayer: a Jesuit preacher in Munich who would not stop preaching when the Nazis forbade it.",
  tryit=["Distinguish freedom from license.", "Run a case through the three sources of morality.", "State the first precept of natural law."],
  saintStory="Rupert Mayer was born in 1876 in Stuttgart, Germany. He became a Jesuit and was ordained in 1899. During World War I, he served as a chaplain on the front lines, where he lost his left leg to a grenade. He returned to Munich, a popular preacher, a pastor to the poor and to migrants, and a friend of workers.\nIn the 1920s he saw the rise of Nazism and denounced it. He preached against its racism, its hatred of the Church, and its violence. From 1937 he was forbidden to preach. He ignored the ban, because his conscience told him he could not be silent. He was arrested in 1937, imprisoned for seven months, then released. When he preached again, he was arrested in 1939 and sent to Sachsenhausen concentration camp. Fearing that his death would make him a martyr, the regime put him under house arrest in a monastery at Ettal.\nHe died on All Saints’ Day, 1945, while saying Mass, of a stroke. He was beatified in 1987.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is a person free if they can do anything they want? Is a fish free if it leaves the water? What does it mean to be truly free?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Freedom is ‘the power, rooted in reason and will, to act or not to act, to do this or that, and so to perform deliberate actions on one’s own responsibility.’ (CCC 1731) It makes us responsible for our acts. The more we do good, the freer we become. Jesus: ‘If you continue in my word, you are truly my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will make you free.’ (John 8:31-32) Freedom is not license, the idea that we can do what we want. Sin enslaves: ‘Everyone who commits sin is a slave to sin.’ (John 8:34) True freedom is freedom for the good. Freedom has limits: facts, others’ rights, the moral law.”", "Say: “The moral law is the work of divine Wisdom. Natural law is the moral law ‘written on the heart’ (Romans 2:14-15), accessible to reason, which expresses the original sense that enables us to discern good and evil. Its first precept: do good and avoid evil. Its principles include protecting life, family, truth, and seeking God. The natural law is universal and unchangeable, and is the basis for human rights. It is distinguished from the Old Law (the Ten Commandments), the New Law (the Gospel, the Beatitudes), and civil law. Thomas Aquinas defined law as ‘an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by the one who has care of the community, and promulgated.’ An unjust law is not truly law (St. Augustine, Martin Luther King Jr. citing Aquinas).”", "Say: “The morality of human acts rests on three sources: the object chosen (what the act is), the intention (the end for which we do it), and the circumstances. For an act to be good, all three must be good. Some acts are intrinsically evil: ‘it is never allowed to do evil that good may come of it’ (Romans 3:8). This rules out, for example, murder of an innocent, adultery, and torture, regardless of intention. Conscience is the judgment by which we apply the law to a particular act. It can be formed or deformed. Mayer’s conscience told him he must preach; the law of the state said otherwise. He appealed to a higher law. Moral objectivity is key: the good is not just what we feel or what the majority says.”"],
    ask=["What is true freedom? (The power to do good, based on truth.)", "What is natural law? (The moral law known by reason, written on the heart.)", "What are the three sources of morality? (Object, intention, circumstances.)"],
    practice=["Run three cases through the three sources: lying to protect a friend; stealing to feed a family; breaking a promise to help someone.", "For each, identify the object, intention and circumstances, and decide.", "Write the difference between freedom and license in your own words.", "Share a case where you struggled."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make me free for the good.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Use the three sources to think through a decision this week.”")),

'11-5-3': dict(
  saint="John Climacus: the abbot whose Ladder of Divine Ascent charts the climb from vice to virtue.",
  tryit=["Match each vice with its remedy.", "Rate yourself honestly.", "Choose one virtue and plan daily practice."],
  saintStory="John was a monk who lived on Mount Sinai in the seventh century, in the monastery of St. Catherine. He entered as a teenager and lived as a hermit for many years in a cell near the foot of the mountain, where he studied the Fathers and prayed. Around the age of seventy-five, he was elected abbot of the monastery.\nAt the request of another abbot, John wrote The Ladder of Divine Ascent, a manual for monks. It describes thirty steps, like the rungs of Jacob’s ladder, from renouncing the world to love. The steps include obedience, repentance, remembrance of death, mourning, freedom from anger, humility, discernment, prayer and love. Along the way, he describes in detail the struggles with vices: vainglory, pride, anger, despair and others.\nHe died around 649. The book became hugely popular in the East, read every Lent in monasteries. His point: the spiritual life is a climb, with falls and recoveries, and virtue is built step by step. The monastic icon shows monks climbing a ladder while demons try to pull them down and angels help them.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a ladder, or draw one. Ask, “What is the difference between falling off a ladder and never climbing one? Which is worse?” Take answers. “John Climacus says the spiritual life is a climb with many falls.”",
    teach=["Say: “Grace is God’s free gift, his help to respond to his call and become his adopted children. The Catechism: ‘Grace is a participation in the life of God.’ (CCC 1997) Sanctifying grace is the habitual gift that makes us pleasing to God. Actual graces are God’s interventions at the start of conversion or in the course of sanctification. Sacramental graces are proper to each sacrament. Charisms are special gifts for the common good. Grace is first of all the gift of the Holy Spirit who justifies and sanctifies us. It is not earned: ‘For by grace you have been saved through faith.’ (Ephesians 2:8) Yet it requires cooperation: grace perfects nature but does not replace it. The saints show that grace can make weak people strong.”", "Say: “Virtue. A virtue is ‘a habitual and firm disposition to do the good’ (CCC 1803). Human virtues are acquired by education, deliberate acts and perseverance. The four cardinal virtues: prudence (right reason in action), justice (giving to God and neighbor what is due), fortitude (firmness in difficulty), temperance (moderation of the attraction of pleasure). They are the hinges (cardo) of moral life. The three theological virtues, faith, hope and charity, are infused by God and have God as their object. They are given with grace. The gifts of the Holy Spirit and the fruits follow. Aristotle said we become just by doing just acts. Aquinas held that grace elevates virtue.”", "Say: “Sin and vice. Vices are habits that incline us toward evil. The capital sins are pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony and sloth. Pride is the root. Each vice has an opposite virtue and remedy: pride and humility; avarice and generosity; envy and gratitude; wrath and patience; lust and chastity; gluttony and temperance; sloth and diligence (or zeal). Evagrius first described eight thoughts; Gregory the Great gave the list of seven. Practical program: examine daily; target a particular vice; do the opposite virtue in small acts; use the sacraments; accept correction; ask for help. Growth is slow: ‘Slowly, slowly, the light comes.’ The Ladder shows that falls are expected, and the key is to get up and go on.”"],
    ask=["What is grace? (Participation in God’s life; his free gift.)", "What are the cardinal virtues? (Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance.)", "What is the root of all the capital sins? (Pride.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: vice, opposite virtue, specific daily action, who can help.", "Fill in for all seven capital sins.", "Honestly rate yourself 1-5 on each virtue.", "Choose one to work on this month, with a daily action."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me the grace to climb.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Track one virtue for a week.”")),
}
